---
title: "CLI reference"
---

*Reference: CLI · [Front matter](frontmatter.tmd) · [Cell options](cell-options.tmd) · [Configuration](configuration.tmd) · [Shortcodes](shortcodes.tmd) · [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.tmd)*

| Command | Effect |
|---|---|
| `preview <file.tmd> [port]` | Live preview server (default port 4321; aliases `dev`, `serve`) |
| `preview <dir>` | Live **multi-page site/book** preview (cross-page nav + per-page hot reload) |
| `build <file.tmd> [out.html]` | Write a self-contained HTML file (default `<name>.html`) |
| `build <file.tmd> --out <dir>` | Write `<dir>/index.html` + copied assets (a portable folder) |
| `build <file.tmd> --bare` | Write a **bare** page: zero `<script>`, zero CDN, CSS-only theme (drafts / archives / print input) |
| `build <dir> [--out <dir>]` | Build a **multi-page site/book** to `_site/` / `_book/` |
| `publish <dir> [--project-name <name>] [--dry-run]` | Build a site/book + deploy it to Cloudflare Pages behind a shared passcode (Wrangler direct upload) |
| `render <file.tmd>` | Render a one-shot full HTML page to stdout |
| `blocks <file.tmd>` | List block ids and sourcepos (for debugging) |
| `schema [--out <dir>]` | Emit the bundled JSON Schemas for `_site.yml` + front matter (editor autocomplete) |
| `check <file.tmd\|dir> [--format human\|json]` | List every located diagnostic and exit non-zero if any (a CI / pre-publish gate) |
| `init [dir]` | Scaffold a minimal previewable site (`_site.yml` + `index.tmd`) in `dir` (default `.`); refuses to overwrite |
| `new <post\|page\|deck> <slug>` | Scaffold one document, correct on its first save; refuses to overwrite |
| `symbols <file.tmd> [--format human\|json]` | List the document's cross-reference targets (what you can write after `@`) |
| `completions <bash\|zsh\|fish>` | Print a shell completion script to stdout (command names + file arguments) |

`check` renders the document or site in memory and reports everything that flows
through the diagnostics channel: unknown front-matter / `_site.yml` / cell-option /
container keys (with did-you-mean), unknown callout kinds, broken `@xref`
cross-references, unknown shortcodes, a missing `bibliography:`, duplicate heading
`{#id}`s, broken in-page `#anchor` links, and opt-in `prose-lint:` findings. It also
catches these **publish-blocking** mistakes:

| Rule | Flags |
|---|---|
| Broken relative link | A `[text](other.tmd)` / `[text](page.html#x)` whose **local target file** is missing. In a site, links resolve against the page registry (`.tmd`→`.html`), so a cross-page anchor `page.html#frag` whose `frag` is no id on that page is flagged too. **External `http(s)://` links are never checked**: `check` stays offline and deterministic, and a `{{< embed >}}` deck or a cross-book / mounted link (one that climbs above the site root) is left alone. |
| Missing local video | A `{{< video clip.mp4 >}}` (or a raw `<video src>` / `<video><source></video>`) whose local file is absent (the video sibling of the local-image check). `<audio>` sources are skipped (often streamed/generated). |
| Dangling reactive input | A `{js}` cell's `//\| input: x` that no `{js}` cell (`//\| name`/`//\| viewof`) or `{{< input >}}` control defines, with a did-you-mean over the known names. Suppressed when the doc has a Python/R cell (a runtime `define` may publish the name). |
| Reactive dependency cycle | A loop among `{js}` cells (`a` consumes `b`, `b` consumes `a`), statically mirroring the runtime graph so none would run. |
| Heading-level skip | A heading that jumps two or more levels deeper than the one before it (an `h2` directly followed by an `h4`, skipping `h3`), which breaks the document outline for screen readers. Only a true *mid-document* skip is flagged (never "doesn't start at `h1`"), and slide decks are skipped (they are slide-structured). The static half of the live preview's a11y audit. |
| Missing alt text | A hand-written / passthrough `<img>` with no `alt` attribute, so a screen reader can't describe it (a decorative image should set `alt=""`). Markdown `![…](…)` always emits an `alt`, so this catches raw `<img>` only. |
| No accessible name | An `<a href>` / `<button>` in content with empty text and no `aria-label` / `title` / labelled image (e.g. an icon-only link), which a screen reader announces as just "link" / "button". |

It does **not** execute code cells (it is fast, kernel-free,
and deterministic, so runtime cell errors stay in `preview`/`build`). Output is
`path:line: message` by default, or a `[{file, line, message}]` array with
`--format json`. It exits `0` when clean and `1` when any diagnostic is found, so it
drops straight into CI or a pre-commit hook:

```sh
taliesin check .                          # fails (exit 1) if any page has a diagnostic
taliesin check . --format json > problems.json
```

`--format json` stays valid JSON even when `check` itself can't run: an unreadable or
missing path (or an empty site) is reported as a single `{"error": "…"}` object on
**stdout** (with a non-zero exit), so `taliesin check x --format json | jq` never chokes
on a stray human message. Human format keeps that error on stderr.

At a glance, here is everything `check` looks at, grouped by what it protects:

| Category | Catches |
|---|---|
| **Config typos** | Unknown front-matter, `_site.yml`, cell-option, and `:::` container keys (with did-you-mean), unknown callout kinds, unknown shortcodes, YAML parse errors |
| **Cross-references** | Broken `@fig-` / `@sec-` / `@eq-` / `@lst-` / `@tbl-` refs (no matching label), a citation with no `bibliography:` |
| **Links & anchors** | Broken relative `.tmd`/`.html` links (and, in a site, cross-page links + cross-page `#anchor`s), broken in-page `#anchor`s, duplicate heading `{#id}`s |
| **Local media** | Missing local images (`src`/`href`) and `{{< video >}}`/`<video>` files |
| **Reactive `{js}`** | A `//\| input:` naming nothing that's defined, a dependency cycle among `{js}` cells |
| **Accessibility** | An `<img>` with no `alt`, a mid-document heading-level skip (decks exempt), an `<a>`/`<button>` with no accessible name |
| **Opt-in** | `prose-lint:` banned-word findings |

External `http(s)://` links are never fetched (`check` stays offline and
deterministic), and it never executes code cells. On its own, `check` exits `1` if it
finds **anything** in any of those categories; it is the kernel-free gate.

`--strict` is a separate flag on **`build`** (not on `check`). It promotes a normally
non-fatal build into a hard failure: the page is still written, but `build` exits
non-zero if **any located warning** fired or **any code cell errored** at runtime.
Because `build` does run cells, `--strict` adds the one thing `check` structurally
can't see (a cell that *throws*) on top of the located diagnostics, which makes
`taliesin check . && taliesin build . --strict` a thorough two-stage CI gate: `check`
fails fast and kernel-free on structure, then a strict `build` proves every cell
actually runs.

`--version` / `-V` prints the version followed by the git commit it was built from,
in parentheses:

```text
Taliesin 0.2.0 (cdf0a13)
```

`--help` / `-h` (or no arguments) prints the
top-level usage. Putting `--help` / `-h` **after** a subcommand
(`taliesin build --help`, `taliesin check -h`, …) prints a focused page for just that
command: its synopsis, its flags, and a one-line example. If
the default port (or one you pass) is already in use, `preview` auto-picks the next free
port and logs the chosen URL.

`preview` takes these flags (they may appear anywhere on the line):

| Flag | Effect |
|---|---|
| `--host` | Bind your LAN and print a **QR code** for phones, gated by a per-session access token (see below) |
| `--open` | Launch the default browser at the preview URL |
| `--no-exec` | Render code cells as source without executing them (preview untrusted docs safely) |

`build` takes these flags (they may appear anywhere on the line):

| Flag | Effect |
|---|---|
| `--out <dir>` (alias `--dir`) | Write a portable `<dir>/index.html` + copied assets instead of a single file |
| `--strict` | Exit non-zero if any cell errored or any located warning fired (a CI gate; the page is still written) |
| `--bare` | Single-doc only: emit a **bare** page (zero `<script>`, zero CDN, CSS-only theming) |
| `--jobs <N>` (alias `-j`) | Maximum pages to build in parallel (default: auto, memory- and core-capped; `--jobs 1` forces sequential; `--jobs 0` same as auto) |

A regular `build` already content-gates the optional enhancer scripts (mermaid, `{js}`,
walkthrough, tabset, scrolly) to the constructs a page actually uses, so a prose page
ships less JavaScript without losing any feature. `--bare` goes further for a
single document: it ships **no** `<script>` at all and themes purely with CSS (a forced
`theme:` is hard-coded; otherwise it follows the OS via `prefers-color-scheme`).
Server-rendered math still works; `{js}` cells, Mermaid rendering, and the
copy/lightbox/reader controls are dropped (each warned, never silently). A slide deck or
a `<dir>` site is refused (their navigation needs JavaScript). A `build` skips code
execution only through the `TALIESIN_NO_EXEC=1` environment variable, not a `--no-exec`
flag.

A `--bare` page is still **responsive**: the viewport meta tag and every CSS media
query ship as usual, so it reflows on a phone and respects `prefers-color-scheme`. It
just has no behaviour that needs scripting. Because `{js}` cells and Mermaid both
render in the browser, a `--bare` build empties them (and warns once per kind) rather
than shipping a broken placeholder, so reach for `--bare` on prose, math, and
server-executed figures, not on pages built around live `{js}` or diagrams.

### Portable, offline builds (email the whole thing as a zip)

A `--out <dir>` build (single doc, site, or book) is a **self-contained folder**: open
`index.html` straight from disk (`file://`), or zip the folder and send it. Everything
the reader needs is copied in and works with no server and no network, **offline**:
navigation and prev/next, the Cmd-K search across the whole book, all math (rendered to
KaTeX at build time with the fonts inlined), the reading enhancers (theme toggle,
focus mode, the mobile pull-up table of contents), and any `{js}` cells (the
`d3` / `Plot` runtimes are vendored, not fetched). Click-to-source is *not* shipped into
a build (it belongs to the live preview), so a reader never gets a dead outline.

The **one** external dependency is **Mermaid diagrams**, which load the Mermaid runtime
from a CDN by default. For a truly offline book, point `TALIESIN_MERMAID_URL` at a local
copy of `mermaid.min.js` (copied into the folder) before building, or use `--bare` /
server-rendered figures on pages that don't need live diagrams. (Everything else is
already local.)

### Publishing & sharing

A `--out <dir>` folder (a single doc, a site, or a book) is **plain static HTML** — no
server runtime, no build step to reproduce, no database. So *any* way of serving static
files publishes it. Pick whichever fits how you already work:

- **Send it directly.** Zip the folder and email it, or drop it in a shared drive. The
  recipient opens `index.html` from disk — everything works offline (see above). Best for
  a draft to a supervisor or a co-author who just needs to read it.
- **GitHub Pages** (free, good for a book/site tied to a repo). Build into a folder, then
  publish that folder to a `gh-pages` branch (or commit it to `/docs` on your default
  branch) and enable Pages in the repo settings:

  ```sh
  taliesin build mybook --out _site       # build the static site/book
  cd _site
  git init -q && git add -A && git commit -qm "publish"
  git push -f git@github.com:you/mybook.git HEAD:gh-pages   # a throwaway history is fine
  ```

  (Add a `.nojekyll` file to the folder so GitHub serves the `_`-prefixed asset dirs.)
- **A drag-and-drop host** (Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, Vercel, Surge). Point it at the
  `--out` folder as the publish directory, or drag the folder into their web uploader.
  Nothing to configure — it's already built.
- **Your own server / object storage.** `rsync -a _site/ you@host:/var/www/mybook/`, or
  `aws s3 sync _site s3://your-bucket` behind a CDN. It's just files.

**Set `url:` in `_site.yml` before publishing to a real domain.** The absolute-URL
`<meta>` tags (Open Graph / Twitter card image, canonical link) are only emitted when a
`url:` is configured — `taliesin check` warns if you set a social `image:` without one.
Run `taliesin check <dir>` first: a green check means no broken links, missing anchors, or
other publish-blocking mistakes ship to your readers.

### `taliesin publish`: one command to a passcode-gated URL

For a private draft you want a supervisor or co-author to read at a stable URL,
`taliesin publish <dir>` builds the site or book, gates it behind a shared passcode,
and deploys it to [Cloudflare Pages](https://pages.cloudflare.com/) (free, works with a
private repo). The passcode is stored as a Cloudflare secret, never in your repo, and the
flow is one-way: publish never writes back to your source.

One-time setup per project (needs [Wrangler](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/wrangler/),
Cloudflare's CLI, and a Cloudflare API token with the Pages:Edit permission):

```sh
export CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=...      # also CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
wrangler pages project create my-book --production-branch production
wrangler pages secret put PASSWORD --project-name my-book   # type the passcode once
```

Then, every time you want to update the live copy:

```sh
taliesin publish .                   # build, gate, deploy; prints https://my-book.pages.dev
taliesin publish . --dry-run         # build + gate + print the deploy command, no deploy
```

The Cloudflare project name defaults to a slug of the directory name; override it with
`--project-name` or a `publish:` block in `_site.yml`:

```yaml
publish:
  provider: cloudflare
  project: my-book
```

Your readers open the URL, type the passcode once in the browser's password prompt (no
account needed), and see the current build. A shared passcode keeps casual strangers out;
it is forwardable and is not per-person access control, which is the right level for a
work-in-progress draft to a supervisor. Note that a book using live Mermaid diagrams
still loads Mermaid from a CDN at view time (everything else is offline); this is harmless
behind the gate.

## Starting from something: `init` and `new`

`init` scaffolds a whole site (`_site.yml` + `index.tmd`). `new` scaffolds a single
document inside one:

```sh
taliesin new post my-first-post   # posts/my-first-post/index.tmd, dated today
taliesin new page about           # about.tmd
taliesin new deck my-talk         # my-talk.tmd, with `format: deck`
```

The scaffold is correct on its first save: it renders, and `taliesin check` passes on it
with no diagnostics. That is not a promise, it is a test. What each `new` writes is pinned
byte-for-byte by `corpus/scaffold/`, which the corpus regression net renders and lints like
any other document, so a front-matter key the validator would reject cannot survive in the
scaffold. A slug must be lowercase letters, digits and hyphens (it becomes the page's URL),
and like `init`, `new` refuses to overwrite an existing file.

## Inspecting a document: `render`, `blocks`, and `symbols`

Three commands print to a terminal instead of starting a server, which is handy for
piping, diffing, and debugging.

`render` writes one complete, self-contained HTML page to **stdout**. It is the
non-interactive twin of a single-file `build`, so you can redirect or pipe it:

```sh
taliesin render post.tmd > post.html
taliesin render post.tmd | grep -c data-block-id   # count emitted blocks
```

Unlike `build` and `preview`, `render` **never starts a kernel**: `{python}` and
`{r}` cells emit as highlighted source with an empty output block (no plots, and any
`@fig-` reference to a computed figure dangles), so it warns when it sees kernel cells:

```text
warn: render does not execute code cells (2 kernel cells emitted as source;
      figures/outputs will be empty). Use `build` or `preview` to run them.
```

`{js}` cells are fine, since they run in the browser. Use `build`/`preview` when you
need the executed outputs.

`blocks` is a debug view of the block model: for each block it prints the
content-hash id, the `line:col-line:col` sourcepos, the origin file (`(primary)` or
an `{{< include >}}`d path), and a 64-character text preview of the rendered HTML.
It does not execute anything. The shape is one aligned row per block:

```text
title: Some("Hello")
3 block(s)

id                sourcepos       source-file             preview
a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8  1:1-1:14        (primary)               Hello
0f9e8d7c6b5a4039  3:1-5:32        (primary)               A short paragraph that introduces…
7c6b5a4039f0e1d2  7:1-9:3         _includes/note.tmd      Note: included from another file
```

This is the same `data-block-id` + `data-sourcepos` that drive click-to-source and
incremental updates, so `blocks` is the way to confirm a block carries the source map
you expect (and which file it came from) without opening the browser dev tools.

`symbols` lists every cross-reference target the document defines: the anchors you can
write after `@`, each with the number Taliesin resolved for it.

```text
$ taliesin symbols post.tmd
fig-scree                     fig    1
sec-why                       sec    1
tbl-summary                   tbl    1
```

The point is that it sees **both** ways an anchor can be written: the brace form
(`## Why it works {#sec-why}`) and the cell form (`#| label: fig-scree`). A regex over
the source can only find the first, which is why the VS Code companion asks the binary
instead of guessing: `--format json` is what feeds its `@`-completion.

```sh
taliesin symbols post.tmd --format json | jq '.[].id'
```

Like `render` and `blocks`, it renders in memory and **never starts a kernel**, so an
editor can call it while you type. A cell's `label:` is registered when the block model
is built, long before the cell would run.

`--host` binds `0.0.0.0`, so it gates non-loopback access behind a token baked into
the printed LAN URL and QR (`?t=…`, then a `qmd_token` cookie); a fresh token is
minted each server start, and loopback (`127.0.0.1`) is exempt. See
[Reading on any screen](../using/preview.tmd) for the full behaviour.

Put `target/release/taliesin` on your `PATH` (a symlink, or a small wrapper script
that rebuilds it when the tool's sources change) so `taliesin preview <file>`
works from anywhere.

## Shell completions

`taliesin completions <bash|zsh|fish>` prints a completion script to stdout: it
completes the subcommand as the first word (and the shell name after `completions`),
then falls back to file completion for the document argument. The command list is
generated from the CLI itself, so it can never drift from the commands the tool
actually has (a test pins the two together).

```sh
# bash
taliesin completions bash > ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/taliesin
# zsh (into a directory on your $fpath, then run compinit)
taliesin completions zsh > "${fpath[1]}/_taliesin"
# fish
taliesin completions fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/taliesin.fish
```

## Environment

| Variable | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `TALIESIN_PYTHON` | Python with `ipykernel` for `{python}` cells (default `python3`) |
| `TALIESIN_R` | R with `IRkernel` for `{r}` cells (default `R`) |
| `TALIESIN_CELL_TIMEOUT` | Per-cell wall-clock cap in seconds before interrupt (default 120; `0` disables the cap, but a per-output silent-hang backstop still applies) |
| `TALIESIN_HOST` | Same as `--host` (expose on the LAN) |
| `TALIESIN_OPEN` | Same as `--open` (launch a browser) |
| `TALIESIN_NO_EXEC` | Same as `--no-exec` (never execute code cells) |
| `TALIESIN_NO_CACHE` | Ignore and skip writing the `_freeze/` execution cache |
| `TALIESIN_NO_CLEAR` | Don't clear the terminal on each render (keep scrollback) |
| `TALIESIN_MERMAID_URL` | Override the Mermaid library URL (self-host next to your site for a fully offline build; on load failure a visible `[data-mermaid-error]` banner shows the diagram source) |

Without a kernel, cells render as highlighted source and the preview shows a
"kernel unavailable" diagnostic rather than failing.
